A||||5||olympic||suspension on the 88s||||5
Z||000000||olympic||10-22-1999||03:10 AM||||Does anyone know exactly what the difference is between the suspensions on the 1988 4 cyl coupe, the formula and the GT? Does the coupe also have the lotus designed suspension?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||10-22-1999||06:24 AM||||Hi,<P>All 88s have the Lotus designed suspension.  I'm not sure of what differences there are between the coupe, Formula, and GT but I suspect it is in the spring rates, shocks, and the addition of a rear sway bar on the Formula and GT.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||10-22-1999||07:43 AM||||Actually, NO production Fiero had a Lotus designed suspension.  This was nothing but a media rumour, and is entirely incorrect.  This inaccuracy was actually confirmed by the entire engineering team, production  managers, and the maker himself Hulki Aldikacti, during banquets at various Fiero meets.  <P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||10-22-1999||09:12 AM||||I think a more correct statement would be that Pontiac examined Lotus suspension and redesigned the Fiero Suspension in 88 with some things they learned from Lotus.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||10-22-1999||09:37 AM||||From what I've heard the suspension on the Formula and GT is identicle.  I'm not sure what the difference is on the coupe other than no rear sway bar, but I'm sure it's more than that. How does the ride compare between the two suspensions?||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChadMan||10-22-1999||10:20 AM||||The general consensus seems to be that the Coupe just lacked the rear sway bar; everything else was the same between the 88 Coupe and the Formula/GT setup.<P>But I've always wondered if the spring rates were different.  When I bought my 88 Coupe, it had a lot of sag in the rear springs at 68,000 miles - the tops of the rear wheels leaned in enough (camber?) to wear the inner edge of the tires down to the cord with tread still left on the outer edge.  I replaced the rear springs with Formula springs from the Fiero Store - no more sag.  It would make sense to put higher-rate springs in the back of the V6 cars, wouldn't it?||||reg||1
